---
import type { ExperienceRole } from "../types/emdash";
import CareerEntry from "./CareerEntry.astro";

interface Props {
  roles: ExperienceRole[];
}

const { roles } = Astro.props;
---

<section id="systems" class="scroll-mt-28">
  <header class="max-w-2xl">
    <p class="act-kicker">Act two — Systems</p>
    <h2 class="act-title">Then I started building the thing that made it scale.</h2>

    <div class="mt-7 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-400">
      <p>
        Running operations taught me where the hours go. Reporting rebuilt by hand every month.
        A spreadsheet only one person understood. Integrations nobody had asked for because
        nobody knew they were possible. I automated them, lifted margin 25%, and removed $50K of
        labor a year — and learned that the most useful thing I can do in a room is notice the
        friction everyone has stopped seeing.
      </p>
      <p>
        By 2021 I was back on the front line with a different toolkit. I built a custom GPT with
        the company president that cut job-description drafting from 30–40 minutes to under 10.
        Then a full Claude-powered suite that took a search from intake to live in 90% less time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="path-only mt-8" data-path-for="hiring">
      <p class="path-note">
        What I bring to a team: someone who has carried a quota and built the automation behind
        it. I don&apos;t need a mandate to fix a workflow.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <aside class="path-only mt-8" data-path-for="build">
      <p class="path-note">
        If a process in your company needs three people and a spreadsheet, that is the kind of
        thing I have already done four times.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </header>

  <div class="mt-14 space-y-16">
    {roles.map((role) => <CareerEntry role={role} />)}
  </div>
</section>
